SEITO-RYŪ MASTER CORPUS

Founding Synthesis and Current Canon

Current through 7 October 2026

This document is the first full reconciliation of the seven master sections developed during the founding period of 聖都流: Seito-ryū. It is not a transcription of every earlier draft. It distinguishes the current system from the developmental record and preserves superseded formulations where their history matters.

The editorial rule is deliberate. The formation record was created to preserve the actual order in which ideas developed rather than rewriting the past into an artificially tidy origin story. It also distinguished documented history, Engi, interpretation, and deliberate Seito constructions while allowing unresolved provenance to remain unresolved. Hyorikan_Master_Timeline(1).docx

Accordingly, this Corpus operates at three levels simultaneously. It states what Seito currently teaches. It identifies what is inherited and what Seito has added. It preserves enough developmental provenance that future practitioners can reconstruct how the current form arose.

The foundational archival principle is:

Preserve what was said. Record what was learned. Correct what proved wrong. Reclassify what was misunderstood. Credit what came later. Never rewrite the past to make the present look inevitable.

⸻

I. INSTITUTIONAL ARCHITECTURE

I.1. Founding identity

聖都流: Seito-ryū is a modern martial tradition founded in Charleston, South Carolina, in 2026.

The name is read Seito-ryū, with no long vowel in Seito. 聖都: Seito means Holy City or Sacred City. 流: ryū identifies the continuing stream or tradition.

Seito does not claim that an ancient ryūha called Seito-ryū was secretly transmitted to the present. One of the earliest constitutional statements expressly says that the school does not claim an inherited ryūha where none exists and begins openly in gratitude to the traditions and teachers that preserved its source material. Seito_Ryu_About_Draft_2026-09-18_v4(1).docx

That distinction remains fundamental.

Seito may possess old inheritances, receive old lore, discover old relationships, reconstruct old problems, and create new forms. None of those operations requires the modern institution itself to pretend to be ancient.

The public martial identity may be expressed as:

聖都流合気武道: Seito-ryū Aiki Budō

The present period is:

創流期: Sōryū-ki

the formative period of the ryū.

⸻

I.2. The constitutional problem Seito is designed to solve

Modern martial organizations commonly collapse several different relationships into one hierarchy.

Rank becomes teaching authority.

Teaching authority becomes organizational office.

Office becomes ownership.

Seniority becomes intellectual authority.

Financial support becomes influence.

Technical contribution becomes institutional possession.

Membership becomes presumed transmission.

Seito rejects that collapse.

Its architecture is intentionally multiaxial.

A person can hold high technical rank without possessing House authority. A contributor can produce important intellectual property without becoming a senior martial teacher. A patron can support the institution heavily without buying lineage. A teacher can transmit material without owning the organization. Someone can enter the House relationship without possessing advanced technical proficiency.

The governing principle is:

Access ≠ transmission ≠ teaching authority ≠ office ≠ ownership.

A parallel research rule is:

Record ≠ contribution ≠ attribution ≠ economic right.

Those distinctions should remain visible institutionally rather than being left to custom.

⸻

I.3. The public tradition

聖都流: Seito-ryū is the public martial tradition.

It is the visible Omote identity through which training, curriculum, teaching relationships, public events, publications, and martial culture are presented.

Seito can contain material from multiple source traditions without claiming that all of that material has become Seito property. The founding documents explicitly prefer continuing relationship with a living root over unnecessarily absorbing or renaming inherited material. Seito_Ryu_About_Draft_2026-09-18_v4(1).docx

This means, for example, that Aikikai Aikidō practiced within Seito remains Aikikai Aikidō.

The same principle applies more broadly:

Preserve the root connection. Preserve the name and authority of the source. Identify precisely what Seito adds.

⸻

I.4. 聖都流門人会: Seito-ryū Monjinkai

聖都流門人会: Seito-ryū Monjinkai

Working English name:

Seito Fellowship

This is the broad people-body.

It can include students, practitioners, volunteers, stewards, supporters, families, researchers, teachers, and persons who participate substantially in the life of Seito without necessarily entering every deeper layer.

Monjinkai status does not automatically confer:

technical rank,

Nyūmon,

House membership,

teaching authorization,

transmission,

institutional office,

Aiki Jikkenjō attribution,

economic residuals,

or succession rights.

The Monjinkai is the broad human field in which people can participate, form relationships, develop competence, contribute socially, and potentially move toward deeper responsibility.

⸻

I.5. 入門: Nyūmon and the Gate

入門: Nyūmon means entry through the gate.

Within Seito it is not merely a registration category.

Nyūmon marks entry into a continuing relationship with the tradition and, potentially, the House.

Outside the Gate, Seito can offer ordinary public training, including Aikikai-facing classes governed by recognizable modern dojo norms.

Inside the Gate, the relationship becomes more explicitly that of a martial house: formation, mentorship, community, responsibility, deeper transmission, social belonging, ritual life, intellectual work, and long-term continuity.

Nyūmon opens the path.

It does not complete the path.

It does not automatically grant:

full transmission,

teaching authority,

advanced rank,

research authorship,

ownership,

office,

or succession.

Those are separately earned or granted.

⸻

I.6. The House

The enduring patrimonial entity is currently best referred to simply as:

the House

Its final formal Japanese name remains open.

Earlier developmental vocabulary included:

聖都家: Seito-ke

久家: Kuke

奥家: Oku-ke

Those forms should remain in the archive rather than being retroactively treated as a settled constitutional title for the whole House.

The House is conceptually different from Seito-ryū itself.

Seito-ryū is the martial operating tradition.

The House is the enduring patrimonial body that can accumulate across generations:

property,

archives,

capital,

reputation,

business interests,

ritual objects,

intellectual assets,

transmission records,

and institutional memory.

The House is therefore the long-duration vessel.

Seito-ryū is one of the principal living operations carried through it.

⸻

I.7. 聖都会: Seito-kai and Holy City Society

聖都会: Seito-kai

is associated with Holy City Society.

Current public material explicitly presents Holy City Society as the patron institution rather than simply as another name for the internal Seito House. Seito_Ryu_Full_Site_Code_2026-09-24.docx

Therefore older attempts to canonicalize Seito-kai as the formal name of the entire enduring House are superseded.

The proper current distinction is:

Holy City Society / 聖都会: patron institution

the House: enduring patrimonial body, Japanese formal name still open

Seito-ryū: martial tradition

These may be deeply related without being constitutionally identical.

⸻

I.8. The Aiki Jikkenjō

合気実験場: Aiki Jikkenjō

is the living research and development body.

The September Engi originally defined it as the place where inherited form is progressively returned to uncertainty so that its living operation can be tested. Final_15_Seito_Ryu_Engi_English_Canonical_Accepted_Redline_2026-09-21 2(2).docx

The mature institutional meaning is broader.

The Aiki Jikkenjō is where Seito:

tests inherited methods,

reconstructs incomplete problems,

compares technical approaches,

develops curriculum,

documents failures,

creates experimental forms,

attributes contributions,

investigates Aiki,

and generates new knowledge.

It is not simply a laboratory room.

It is a living body of inquiry.

Membership or participation in the Jikkenjō does not itself create economic rights. Economic attribution attaches to actual documented contribution.

⸻

I.9. Archive, Jikkenjō, Corpus

Three functions must remain distinct.

The Archive preserves what happened.

The Aiki Jikkenjō investigates what may be true or useful.

The Corpus records what the tradition currently accepts and transmits.

The Archive may contain wrong ideas.

The Jikkenjō may contain failed experiments.

The Corpus should contain the current accepted result.

This separation prevents three serious errors:

mistaking old notes for doctrine,

mistaking experiments for canon,

and rewriting developmental history to match current belief.

The founding research documents specifically directed that demonstrations, resistance practice, scenario work, failed experiments, contributions, and reconstructions be distinguished, and that failed experiments remain part of the research record. Seito_Ryu_About_Draft_2026-09-18_v4(1).docx

⸻

I.10. Teaching authority and transmission

師範: Shihan is treated primarily as technical and transmissional authority.

Shihan status does not automatically confer:

ownership of the House,

religious headship,

economic sovereignty,

succession,

control of the patrimony,

or authority over every other institutional domain.

Similarly, a future 派: ha may represent a technical, pedagogical, or transmissional branch without becoming an independent patrimonial sovereignty.

Teaching authority and institutional office are related only where expressly joined.

⸻

I.11. External rank

Rank received from an external tradition remains the rank of that tradition.

Seito does not retroactively convert external rank into a proprietary Seito grade.

Aikikai rank remains Aikikai rank.

Jūdō rank remains Jūdō rank.

Daitō transmission, where actually received, remains attributable to the Daitō line through which it came.

Seito can recognize competence and authorize Seito functions, but it must not counterfeit another institution’s authority.

⸻

I.12. Contribution and authorship

Every meaningful contribution should eventually identify:

the contributor,

their role,

the source material involved,

the problem being studied,

the contribution actually made,

the conditions under which it was tested,

whether it was direct or mediated,

the rights reserved by the contributor,

the rights granted to Seito,

and whether any residual economic right attaches.

A lineage holder, transmitting teacher, guest teacher, researcher, student experimenter, photographer, editor, or patron need not all have the same legal or intellectual relationship to the result.

The institution should record those differences at the time of contribution rather than litigating memory decades later.

