II. ECONOMIC ARCHITECTURE

II.1. Governing principle

The economic architecture exists to support transmission, people, research, property, continuity, and patrimony.

It does not define martial truth.

The controlling rule is:

Money can support the tradition. Money cannot constitute the tradition.

No amount of patronage should purchase:

rank,

lineage,

Nyūmon,

teaching authority,

menkyo,

historical status,

authorship,

or spiritual standing.

This firewall is structural, not merely ethical.

⸻

II.2. The public four-part stack

The current public economic and institutional stack is:

Form	Reading	Function
聖都流	Seito-ryū	Public / Omote identity
合気領家	Aiki Ryōke	Patrons
合気両	Aiki Ryō	Circulating money / token
合気実験場	Aiki Jikkenjō	Living research and development body

This short public stack supersedes longer formulations that placed 聖都流 before every institutional term.

⸻

II.3. 合気領家: Aiki Ryōke

合気領家: Aiki Ryōke

is the patron body.

Patrons may fund:

teacher support,

research,

publications,

property,

equipment,

archives,

scholarships,

horses or martial infrastructure,

business development,

cultural programming,

and other institutional projects.

Patronage can create financial claims where a specific project has been structured that way.

It does not create martial authority.

The preferred model is therefore project-specific capital, not ownership of the entire House.

⸻

II.4. 合気両: Aiki Ryō

合気両: Aiki Ryō

is the circulating monetary or token layer.

Its function is transfer and settlement.

It is not a governance credential.

It is not a rank.

It is not proof of contribution.

It is not lineage.

Its conceptual value lies precisely in separating transferable money from non-transferable human and institutional relationships.

A person can hold Aiki Ryō without being a member of the House.

A senior teacher can possess no meaningful amount of Aiki Ryō and remain senior.

That is intentional.

⸻

II.5. Four different economic flows

The Corpus distinguishes at least four major economic functions.

Patron capital funds particular undertakings.

Operating revenue pays for current institutional activity.

Personal support supports a teacher, practitioner, resident, scholar, or other person.

Stewardship or patrimonial capital builds long-duration House assets.

These should not be casually intermixed.

Someone receiving personal support does not necessarily own the capital that generated the support.

Someone controlling a project budget does not thereby own the institution.

Purpose-bound stewardship is distinct from personal property.

⸻

II.6. Koku, Kokudaka, Roku

Three older Japanese economic concepts remain useful as analogical institutional vocabulary.

石: Koku denotes productive or support capacity.

石高: Kokudaka denotes assessed support capacity or level.

禄: Roku denotes actual stipend or delivered support.

In Seito these are not literal rice-tax measures.

The conceptual distinction is:

Aiki Ryō: transferable monetary value

Koku: support capacity

Kokudaka: assessed level of support

Roku: actual support delivered

This remains a working economic architecture rather than a completed accounting system.

⸻

II.7. Contributor residuals

Aiki Jikkenjō membership does not automatically entitle a participant to a share of all output.

Residual economic rights should attach granularly.

A documented contribution may create a residual where appropriate.

The relevant object is the actual contribution:

a method,

curricular structure,

technical discovery,

publication,

research dataset,

image corpus,

course,

software tool,

design,

or commercial product.

The proper chain is:

research → documentation → testing → attribution → acceptance → possible Corpus entry → possible commercialization → contributor residual where applicable

This protects both the House and the contributor.

⸻

II.8. The patrimonial flywheel

The long-term economic cycle is:

Human capital → intellectual and commercial value → operating surplus → patrimony → stronger conditions for people and research

The purpose is not accumulation for its own sake.

Patrimony should produce durable conditions under which the tradition can survive beyond the personality or finances of one generation.

That means eventually owning or controlling meaningful assets rather than remaining permanently dependent on rented classrooms, transient volunteers, and unstable tuition.

⸻

II.9. Economics and hierarchy

Economic contribution and martial standing remain independent.

A patron may become a student.

A student may become a patron.

A contributor may receive residuals.

A teacher may earn salary.

A House member may steward assets.

None of those relationships should be silently translated into another.

The firewall is:

capital does not buy transmission

and equally:

transmission does not automatically grant capital ownership.

